Converting to consumption equivalents
These terms are in utils. As in class (and recitation 2), we convert them into consumption equivalents. With CRRA utility, scaling consumption in every period by \((1+\omega)\) scales every value by \((1+\omega)^{1-\sigma}\). So the total consumption equivalent of moving from \(A\) to \(B\) is \[ 1 + \omega = \left[\frac{W^{B}}{W^{A}}\right]^{1/(1-\sigma)}.\] We can split this multiplicatively as \(1+\omega = (1+\omega_{I})(1+\omega_{R})\) by writing \[\frac{W^{B}}{W^{A}} = \frac{\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^{B})}{\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^{A})}\times\frac{W^{B}/\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^{B})}{W^{A}/\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^{A})}\] which gives \[
\begin{aligned}
1+\omega_{I} &= \left[\frac{\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^{B})}{\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^{A})}\right]^{1/(1-\sigma)} = \frac{\bar{C}^{B}}{\bar{C}^{A}} \qquad &\text{(insurance)}\\
1 + \omega_{R} &= \left[\frac{W^{B}/\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^{B})}{W^{A}/\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^{A})}\right]^{1/(1-\sigma)} \qquad &\text{(redistribution)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The \(\omega_{I}\) term is the welfare effect of changes in insurance (how the average certainty equivalent changes). The \(\omega_{R}\) term represents changes in the equality (or inequality) of the distribution of certainty equivalents.

Example 2: The decomposition


Now we decompose: \[
\begin{aligned}
W_{1} - W_{0} &= \frac{\log(C^*) - (0.5\mu_{L} + 0.5\mu_{H})}{(1-\beta)} \\
&= \frac{\log(C^*) - \log(\bar{C})}{1-\beta} + \frac{\log(\bar{C}) - (0.5\mu_{L} + 0.5\mu_{H})}{1-\beta} \\
&= \text{insurance} + \text{redistribution}
\end{aligned}
\]
Plugging in \(C^*\) and \(\bar{C}\): \[
\begin{aligned}
\text{insurance} &\propto \log\left(0.5\exp(\mu_{L}+0.5\sigma^2) + 0.5\exp(\mu_{H}+0.5\sigma^2)\right) \\
&\qquad - \log(0.5\exp(\mu_{L}) + 0.5\exp(\mu_{H})) \\
\text{redistribution} &\propto \log(0.5\exp(\mu_{L}) + 0.5\exp(\mu_{H})) - (0.5\mu_{L} + 0.5\mu_{H})
\end{aligned}
\]
Note what happens as \(\mu_{L}\rightarrow\mu_{H}\) and as \(\sigma^2\rightarrow 0\). Intuitive!

Some Cleaning Steps

using DataFrames, CSV, StatsPlots, DataFramesMeta, StatsBase
pce = @select(CSV.read("../data/PCE_index.csv",DataFrame),
              :INCYEAR = :YEAR, :PCE)

data = @chain CSV.read("../data/cps_00049.csv",DataFrame) begin
  @subset coalesce.(:HFLAG,1) .== 1 #&lt;- 2014: keep redesigned sample
  @transform :INCYEAR = :YEAR .- 1 #&lt;- income refers to the previous year
  @subset :INCWAGE .&gt;0 #&lt;- keep only if positive earnings
  @subset :WKSWORK1 .&gt;10 #&lt;- keep only if worked more than 10 weeks
  @transform :HOURS = :UHRSWORKLY .* :WKSWORK1 #&lt;- calculate annual hours
  innerjoin(pce,on=:INCYEAR) #&lt;- deflate with income-year PCE
  @transform :INCWAGE = 100 * :INCWAGE ./ :PCE #&lt;- deflate (2017 base)
  @transform :WAGE = :INCWAGE ./ :HOURS #&lt;- annual earnings / annual hours
end

